        ],
        "title": "Find the Derivative",
        "text": "Next, we take the derivative of f(u) in terms of u, $$\\frac{dy}{du}$$, and the derivative of g(x) in terms of $$x$$, $$\\frac{du}{dx}$$. Multiplying these two expressions together yields our final answer, $$\\frac{dy}{dx}$$. Note that $$g(x)=\\operatorname{cot}\\left(4x+1\\right)$$ is itself a composite function, so use the chain rule again to find $$\\frac{du}{dx}$$.",
        "variabilization": {},
